Author: Ridson Al Farizal P [aut, cre, cph] (ORCID: <https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0617-0214>), Azka Ubaidillah [aut] (ORCID: <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3597-0459>)
Maintainer: Ridson Al Farizal P <alfrzlp@gmail.com>
Description: High-performance Small Area Estimation (SAE) in R, implementing
    frequentist EBLUP models via compiled C++ and Bayesian hierarchical models
    via INLA (Integrated Nested Laplace Approximations). Frequentist models
    include the area-level Fay-Herriot model (Fay and Herriot, 1979
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1979.10482505>), spatial Fay-Herriot (Pratesi and
    Salvati, 2008 <doi:10.1007/s10260-007-0061-9>), spatio-temporal
    Fay-Herriot (Marhuenda et al., 2013 <doi:10.1016/j.csda.2012.09.002>),
    unit-level Battese-Harter-Fuller (Battese et al., 1988
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1988.10478561>), and two-fold subarea models (Torabi
    and Rao, 2014 <doi:10.1016/j.jmva.2014.02.001>) for nested area-subarea
    hierarchies. Bayesian models use INLA (Rue et al., 2009), supporting 6
    likelihood families (Gaussian, Beta, Binomial, Poisson, Negative Binomial,
    Gamma), spatial structures (BYM2, Besag ICAR), temporal dynamics (RW1, RW2,
    AR1), and spatio-temporal interactions. Key features include analytical and
    bootstrap MSE estimation, benchmarking to aggregate targets (Rao and Molina,
    2015 <doi:10.1002/9781118735855>), automatic handling of unsampled domains,
    and S3 methods for summaries, diagnostics and plots.
